<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
 ******************************************************************************
 **  @file   smsi.wxs
 **  @author slspencer
 **  @date   July 22, 2026
 **
 **  @brief
 **  WiX (v5/v6) authoring for the Windows MSI installer of the Seamly app
 **  family: seamly2d (pattern drafting), seamlyme (measurements) and
 **  SeamlyLayout (pattern layout).
 **
 **  Build with `scripts\packaging\windows\smsi.ps1` (local)
 **  or with `ci.yml`'s `windows-msi` job (origin).
 **
 **  (CI). One MSI is produced per architecture from this same source:
 **     Windows x64:    `wix build -arch x64`
 **     Windows arm64:  `wix build -arch arm64`
 **
 **  SOURCE LAYOUT (split 2026-08-15)
 **    smsi.wxs            THIS FILE. <Package> and everything that must be
 **                        inside it: identity, upgrade, ARP, the properties
 **                        the dialogs read, the launch conditions, the
 **                        user-data copy action, and the references that pull
 **                        the fragments in.
 **    smsi_ui.wxs         the wizard: its dialogs and every transition.
 **    smsi_legacy.wxs     finding and removing the pre-MSI installation.
 **    smsi_files.wxs      directory tree, executables, Start Menu shortcuts,
 **                        file associations.
 **    smsi_shortcuts.wxs  optional desktop shortcuts, install-info registry.
 **
 **  EVERY ONE OF THOSE FILES MUST BE PASSED TO `wix build`, and each fragment
 **  must stay referenced from this file. `wix build` links what it is handed
 **  and says nothing about a fragment it never saw or nobody referenced - the
 **  MSI builds and quietly lacks that whole area. smsi.ps1 globs the directory
 **  for exactly this reason, and smsi_check_authoring.ps1 reads the built MSI
 **  to prove the rows are there.
 **
 **  INSTALL LAYOUT
 **    [ProgramFiles64Folder]\SeamlyApps\    seamly2d.exe, seamlyme.exe,
 **                                          SeamlyLayout.exe and the ONE
 **                                          Qt runtime all three share
 **                                          (the merged windeployqt output
 **                                          of all three, incl. the
 **                                          QML modules and
 **                                          QtWebEngineProcess.exe
 **                                          SeamlyLayout needs, plus the
 **                                          MSVC CRT).
 **
 **                                          All three apps build against Qt 6.11.1, so one
 **                                          runtime serves them all.
 **                                          seamly2d still resolves the exe through
 **                                          SeamlyFamilyPaths::locateSeamlyLayout()
 **                                          (src/libs/vmisc/seamly_family_paths.cpp),
 **                                          which checks the flat layout
 **                                          first and keeps the old
 **                                          subdirectory layout as a
 **                                          fallback for installs made by
 **                                          an earlier MSI.
 **
 **  USER DATA IS NEVER DELETED OR OVERWRITTEN:
 **    Per-user settings live under %LOCALAPPDATA%\Seamly\<app>
 **    (AppData\Local\Seamly\Seamly2D, ...\SeamlyMe, ...\SeamlyLayout) plus the shared
 **    %APPDATA%\Seamly\qt6_common.ini. The installer never touches those.
 **
 **    Patterns, measurements and templates live in the USER DATA ROOT, which
 **    Task InstWinX64.1.2 made a choice the user makes during setup:
 **      - SEAMLYDATAROOT holds the folder, default C:\Users\<user>\SeamlyData.
 **      - SeamlyDataDirDlg asks for it; any drive is allowed, including USB and
 **        cloud-synced folders (unlike the program folder, which rejects them).
 **      - The chosen path is recorded in HKLM\SOFTWARE\Seamly\Seamly2D\DataRoot
 **        for the apps to read on first run.
 **      - SeamlyDataMigrateDlg offers to COPY existing work into it. The copy is
 **        opt-in, additive only, and never overwrites: a file already present at
 **        the destination is left exactly as it is. Source files are only read.
 **
 **    Uninstall removes no user data of any kind, so uninstall/reinstall and
 **    upgrade never lose work. This is what the Task 51 "existing installation"
 **    dialog below tells the user, in those words, before it replaces anything.
 **
 **  INSTALL-TIME EXPERIENCE
 **    The package defines its own dialog set instead of using WixUI_InstallDir,
 **    so it owns every page transition. Fresh-install order:
 **
 **      WelcomeDlg -> LicenseAgreementDlg -> SeamlyPreviousInstallDlg ->
 **      InstallDirDlg -> SeamlyDataDirDlg -> SeamlyDataMigrateDlg ->
 **      SeamlyShortcutsDlg -> VerifyReadyDlg -> ProgressDlg -> ExitDialog
 **
 **    SeamlyPreviousInstallDlg  shown only when an earlier install is detected
 **                              — either an older MSI of this product
 **                              (WIX_UPGRADE_DETECTED, set by MajorUpgrade) or
 **                              the old NSIS installation
 **                              (SEAMLYLEGACYUNINSTALLSTRING, found by the
 **                              registry search below). Warns that the program
 **                              files will be replaced and states plainly that
 **                              user data is not touched.
 **    SeamlyDataDirDlg          where the user keeps patterns and measurements.
 **    SeamlyDataMigrateDlg      the opt-in copy of existing work.
 **    SeamlyShortcutsDlg        the optional-desktop-shortcut checkbox.
 **    Every other page is a stock dialog, reused unchanged — see the comments
 **    at the <UI> element.
 **
 **  PREPROCESSOR VARIABLES (passed by smsi.ps1 via -d)
 **    ProductVersion       numeric MSI version x.y.z (see smsi.ps1 for the
 **                         mapping from the project version — MSI ignores the
 **                         4th field for upgrades so YY.M.D.MMMM is folded to
 **                         YY.M.((D-1)*1440 + MMMM) in smsi.ps1
 **    DisplayVersion       full project version string (YY.M.D.MMMM)
 **    RepoRoot             absolute path of the repository root
 **    ParentStagingDir     staged shared runtime tree for every app in the
 **                         package (no exes) — seamly2d/seamlylayout/seamlyme windeployqt
 **                         output -
 **    ExeStagingDir        staged directory holding the app executables
 **
 **
 **  UPGRADES
 **    The UpgradeCode below is FIXED for the lifetime of the product family
 **    and shared by the x64 and arm64 packages: MajorUpgrade removes any older
 **    install (either architecture) before the new one lands, so newer
 **    versions upgrade in place. Never change the UpgradeCode; the per-build
 **    ProductCode is auto-generated on every build.
 **
 **  @copyright
 **  This source code is part of the Seamly project, a suite of apparel CAD
 **  software.
 **  Copyright (C) 2026 Seamly Project
 **  <https://github.com/fashionfreedom/seamly2d> All Rights Reserved.
 **
 **  @license
 **  Seamly2D/SeamlyMe is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify
 **  it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
 **  the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or
 **  (at your option) any later version.
 **
 **  Seamly2D/SeamlyMe is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,
 **  but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
 **  MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the
 **  GNU General Public License for more details.
 **
 **  You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License
 **  along with Seamly2D/SeamlyMe.  If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
 **
 **  SeamlyLayout is licensed under the MIT license.
 ******************************************************************************
-->

<!-- Fail fast with a clear message when the build script forgot a define. -->
<?ifndef var.ProductVersion ?>
  <?error ProductVersion is not defined - build via scripts\packaging\windows\smsi.ps1, which passes -d ProductVersion=x.y.z ?>
<?endif?>
<?ifndef var.DisplayVersion ?>
  <?error DisplayVersion is not defined - build via scripts\packaging\windows\smsi.ps1, which passes -d DisplayVersion=YY.M.D.MMMM ?>
<?endif?>
<?ifndef var.RepoRoot ?>
  <?error RepoRoot is not defined - build via scripts\packaging\windows\smsi.ps1, which passes -d RepoRoot=<repo root> ?>
<?endif?>
<?ifndef var.ParentStagingDir ?>
  <?error ParentStagingDir is not defined - build via scripts\packaging\windows\smsi.ps1 ?>
<?endif?>
<?ifndef var.ExeStagingDir ?>
  <?error ExeStagingDir is not defined - build via scripts\packaging\windows\smsi.ps1 ?>
<?endif?>

<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">

  <!-- Manufacturer must be the legal entity named on the EV code-signing
       certificate (CN=Seamly Systems, Inc.). Windows takes the UAC and
       SmartScreen publisher from the Authenticode signature, not from this
       attribute, so any other value makes Apps &amp; Features disagree with the
       elevation prompt. Source-file copyright headers keep "Seamly Project". -->
  <Package Name="Seamly"
           Manufacturer="Seamly Systems, Inc."
           Version="$(var.ProductVersion)"
           UpgradeCode="cbf4b5f1-c32c-4dbb-b385-3ee4a7b30658"
           Language="1033"
           Compressed="yes"
           Scope="perMachine">

    <SummaryInformation Description="Seamly $(var.DisplayVersion) - apparel CAD for drafting, measurements/sizing, and layout" />

    <!-- Newer versions upgrade in place: the old product (any architecture,
         same UpgradeCode) is removed before the new files are installed.
         AllowSameVersionUpgrades keeps rolling same-minute rebuilds working. -->
    <MajorUpgrade AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"
                  DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of [ProductName] is already installed. Uninstall it first to install this older version." />

    <!-- Single embedded cab: one self-contained .msi file. -->
    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" CompressionLevel="high" />

    <!-- Icons for shortcuts and Apps &amp; Features. MSI requires Icon table
         ids used by advertised shortcuts to carry the matching extension, so
         each Id is the icon's own file name — and each Id must be UNIQUE and
         must match what the shortcuts below reference by name. Getting this
         wrong is a link-time failure, not a runtime one: SeamlyLayout's icon
         was declared as a second "seamlyme.ico", which both left SeamlyMe's
         icon ambiguous and left SeamlyLayoutShortcut's Icon="seamlylayout.ico"
         pointing at an identifier that did not exist (error WIX0094). -->
    <Icon Id="seamly2d.ico" SourceFile="$(var.RepoRoot)\scripts\packaging\windows\seamly2d.ico" />
    <Icon Id="seamlyme.ico" SourceFile="$(var.RepoRoot)\scripts\packaging\windows\seamlyme.ico" />
    <Icon Id="seamlylayout.ico" SourceFile="$(var.RepoRoot)\scripts\packaging\windows\seamlylayout.ico" />

    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="seamly2d.ico" />
    <Property Id="ARPHELPLINK" Value="https://seamly.io" />
    <Property Id="ARPURLINFOABOUT" Value="https://seamly.io" />
    <!-- Apps &amp; features shows the numeric MSI ProductVersion (26.y.z) as its
         DisplayVersion, and that cannot be overridden: the RegisterProduct
         standard action writes that value into the Uninstall key *after*
         WriteRegistryValues, so any component-authored override is overwritten.
         The full YY.M.D.MMMM project version therefore reaches the user two
         other ways — the ARP comment here, and HKLM\SOFTWARE\Seamly\Seamly2D
         (DisplayVersion) written by InstallInfoRegistry below. -->
    <Property Id="ARPCOMMENTS" Value="Seamly $(var.DisplayVersion) - apparel CAD pattern drafting (Seamly2D), measurements/sizing (SeamlyMe), and layout (SeamlyLayout)" />

    <!-- Finding the pre-MSI Seamly2D installation and removing it lives in
         smsi_legacy.wxs: the two registry searches, the Start Menu property,
         and the two removal components. This reference is what links that
         fragment - a fragment nothing refers to is dropped without an error,
         and the old product would then be left in place. -->
    <ComponentGroupRef Id="LegacyRemoval" />

    <!-- ================= optional desktop shortcuts ======================= -->
    <!-- Set by the checkbox on SeamlyShortcutsDlg, and overridable on the
         command line for unattended installs:
             msiexec /i Seamly-x64.msi /qn SEAMLYDESKTOPSHORTCUTS=0
         Default 1 (checked), matching the NSIS installer's behaviour of always
         creating shortcuts. Secure="yes" so the value survives the hand-off to
         the elevated server-side sequence that evaluates the component
         conditions. -->
    <Property Id="SEAMLYDESKTOPSHORTCUTS" Value="1" Secure="yes" />

    <!-- ================= user-data root (Task InstWinX64.1.2) ============== -->
    <!-- Where the apps keep patterns, measurements and templates. TWO
         identifiers, because the user picks a place and Setup names the folder:

           SEAMLYDATAPARENT  what the user chooses. Default %USERPROFILE%.
           SEAMLYDATAROOT    SEAMLYDATAPARENT plus the fixed leaf SeamlyData,
                             so the default is C:\Users\<user>\SeamlyData.

         Both are DIRECTORY ids rather than plain properties, for three reasons:
         WixUI's BrowseDlg edits a directory (it browses whatever
         _BrowseProperty names), a public uppercase directory id can be set on
         the msiexec command line for unattended installs, and CostFinalize
         resolves them so [SEAMLYDATAROOT] expands everywhere below.

             msiexec /i seamly-x64.msi /qn SEAMLYDATAPARENT="E:\"
                 -> E:\SeamlyData
             msiexec /i seamly-x64.msi /qn SEAMLYDATAROOT="E:\Patterns"
                 -> exactly that; overrides the composition, leaf and all

         The default is computed in the UI sequence ONLY. The execute sequence
         of a perMachine package runs elevated as SYSTEM, where %USERPROFILE% is
         SYSTEM's own profile - so computing it there would silently put a data
         root under C:\Windows\system32\config\systemprofile. Secure="yes"
         carries the value the user actually chose across to that elevated
         sequence.

         Consequence for silent installs: /qn runs no UI sequence, so nothing
         computes the default. Pass SEAMLYDATAPARENT (or SEAMLYDATAROOT)
         explicitly for an unattended install; if neither is given the apps fall
         back to their own first-run default, which is the behaviour that
         shipped before this task. -->
    <Property Id="SEAMLYDATAROOT" Secure="yes" />
    <Property Id="SEAMLYDATAPARENT" Secure="yes" />
    <!-- The trailing backslash is required, not cosmetic: Windows Installer
         composes a child directory onto the parent's value verbatim, so
         "C:\Users\me" without it gives C:\Users\meSeamlyData. -->
    <SetProperty Id="SEAMLYDATAPARENT"
                 Value="[%USERPROFILE]\"
                 Before="CostFinalize"
                 Sequence="ui"
                 Condition="NOT SEAMLYDATAPARENT" />

    <!-- Set by SeamlyDataMigrateDlg's confirmation. 0 = record the folder but
         copy nothing. The copy is opt-in: it is the user's data, and an
         unattended install must not start moving files nobody asked about. -->
    <Property Id="SEAMLYCOPYUSERDATA" Value="0" Secure="yes" />

    <!-- ================= reject cloud-synced program folders =============== -->
    <!-- InstWinX64.1.1.3. Program files must not live in a folder a sync client
         owns. OneDrive, Dropbox and Google Drive all rename, lock, or replace a
         file with a placeholder while an app has it mapped, which corrupts a
         running install and breaks repair and uninstall. `><` is the Windows
         Installer substring operator, so this matches the sync root anywhere in
         the path.

         This is a Launch condition, not a dialog button check, because it is
         the only mechanism that also blocks a silent install - where no dialog
         exists to press. LaunchConditions runs in both sequences: in the UI
         sequence INSTALLFOLDER still holds the default (no match, no message),
         and in the execute sequence it holds whatever the user chose or passed
         on the command line, which is the value that has to be rejected.

         The user-data root is deliberately NOT checked: InstWinX64.1.2.2
         requires it to accept OneDrive, Google Drive, Dropbox and USB media,
         because syncing your patterns between machines is the point. -->
    <Launch Condition="NOT ((INSTALLFOLDER &gt;&lt; &quot;OneDrive&quot;) OR (INSTALLFOLDER &gt;&lt; &quot;Dropbox&quot;) OR (INSTALLFOLDER &gt;&lt; &quot;Google Drive&quot;) OR (INSTALLFOLDER &gt;&lt; &quot;GoogleDrive&quot;) OR (INSTALLFOLDER &gt;&lt; &quot;iCloud&quot;) OR (INSTALLFOLDER &gt;&lt; &quot;Box Sync&quot;))"
            Message="Seamly cannot be installed in a cloud-synced folder. OneDrive, Dropbox and Google Drive replace files that are in use, which breaks the program and its uninstall. Choose a local or removable drive." />

    <!-- The wizard dialog set and every transition between its pages lives in
         smsi_ui.wxs. This reference is what links it: a WiX fragment nothing
         refers to is dropped without an error, leaving an MSI that builds and
         shows the wrong pages. Do not remove it. -->
    <UIRef Id="SeamlyUI" />

    <!-- ================= copy existing user data (InstWinX64.1.2.4) ======= -->
    <!-- WHY A SCRIPT AND NOT THE MoveFile TABLE
         MSI's own CopyFile/MoveFile rows can only copy within a single
         directory - their wildcards do not recurse - and a user's pattern
         folder has subdirectories. So the copy is a deferred action running the
         PowerShell helper installed above.

         Impersonate="yes" is required, not cosmetic. A deferred action defaults
         to running as SYSTEM in a perMachine package, and SYSTEM cannot read a
         user's OneDrive or mapped drive. Impersonating puts the copy back in
         the account whose files these are, with exactly that account's rights.

         Return="ignore" is deliberate. The copy is a convenience, and the
         source files are never modified, so a failure mid-copy leaves the
         user's originals untouched and the new folder partly filled - harmless,
         and the apps read whatever is there. Failing the install instead would
         roll back a perfectly good program installation over a file-copy
         problem.

         THERE IS NO ROLLBACK ACTION, ON PURPOSE. A rollback would have to
         delete files out of a folder that may already have held the user's own
         work before this install ran, and no rollback action can reliably tell
         which files it put there from which were already present. Deleting user
         data to tidy up a failed install is a worse outcome than leaving a few
         copied files behind. The copy is additive-only and never overwrites, so
         there is nothing whose absence would leave the machine inconsistent.

         Deferred actions cannot read properties, so the command line is handed
         over through the CustomActionData property of the SAME name - the
         standard pattern, and why the SetProperty below is sequenced
         immediately before the action it feeds. -->
    <SetProperty Id="SeamlyCopyUserData"
                 Value="&quot;powershell.exe&quot; -NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File &quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]smsi_migrate_user_data.ps1&quot; -Destination &quot;[SEAMLYDATAROOT]&quot;"
                 Before="SeamlyCopyUserData"
                 Sequence="execute"
                 Condition="SEAMLYCOPYUSERDATA = &quot;1&quot; AND SEAMLYDATAROOT AND NOT Installed" />

    <CustomAction Id="SeamlyCopyUserData"
                  DllEntry="WixQuietExec64"
                  BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_X64"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="yes"
                  Return="ignore" />

    <!-- After InstallFiles, so the helper script exists on disk; after
         WriteRegistryValues so the recorded DataRoot is already correct even if
         the copy is skipped or fails. -->
    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <Custom Action="SeamlyCopyUserData"
              After="WriteRegistryValues"
              Condition="SEAMLYCOPYUSERDATA = &quot;1&quot; AND SEAMLYDATAROOT AND NOT Installed" />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

    <!-- The directory tree, the three executables, their Start Menu shortcuts
         and their file associations are in smsi_files.wxs. The optional
         desktop shortcuts and the install-info registry values are in
         smsi_shortcuts.wxs. These references are what link those fragments -
         a fragment nothing refers to is dropped without an error. -->
    <ComponentGroupRef Id="FamilyExecutables" />
    <ComponentGroupRef Id="InstallInfo" />
    <ComponentGroupRef Id="OptionalDesktopShortcuts" />

  </Package>
</Wix>
